       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. Ibuprofen 4000 mg four times daily is 16 g/day, about five times the maximum daily dose. At that level the risk of serious harm is high, including GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, metabolic acidosis, CNS effects, and seizures. I can't find a clinical scenario where it would be appropriate, and your patient's normal kidney and liver function wouldn't offset that risk.\n\nHere's what I can do:\n\n- **Enter a standard dose right away.** For example, 800 mg PO three times daily, or 600 mg PO four times daily (both 2,400 mg/day). 400 mg PO four times daily is also an option.\n- **Help with a different goal.** If you need stronger analgesia or anti-inflammatory effect than ibuprofen allows, I can enter a multimodal regimen, such as scheduled acetaminophen plus ibuprofen at a standard dose. A pharmacist consult is another option.\n- **Route this through pharmacy.** If you think there's a real indication for this dose, the pharmacy or clinical decision support process is the right place to document and verify it. I can't do that from here.\n\nTell me which of these you'd like."
      }
